Zero firmware call7 verification, 2026-11-20

The compiler now permits call7 on x86-64 raw and UEFI targets as well as
the existing IA32 raw and UEFI targets. It does not permit call7 in Linux,
Windows, ZEROAPP1 or real-mode source. Normal five-argument call is unchanged.

The x64 operation evaluates the address and seven arguments left-to-right.
RCX, RDX, R8 and R9 carry arguments one through four; the other three follow
the 32-byte shadow space. The pre-call stack is aligned to 16 bytes. R12 is
saved while tracking the original operand stack, then restored; R13 and
the other Microsoft x64 nonvolatile registers follow the callee contract.
The complete 64-bit return value is preserved. IA32 encoding is unchanged.

Reproducible own-Zero fixtures

native-driver.zero is a Linux host program. Compile with --host. Compile
raw64-probe.zero with --raw and supply that binary to the driver on stdin.
The driver allocates writable memory, installs an original callback encoded
as Zero data, and makes both code pages executable before running the probe.
No external assembler, compiler runtime or copied machine-code implementation
is used. These test callbacks are authored for their specific test contract.

The native probe runs 128 rounds. The callback records scalar arguments two
through seven into the buffer supplied as argument one, checks incoming
stack alignment, overwrites all legal shadow-space slots, and returns a
wide 64-bit value. The probe verifies pending arithmetic operands, nested
calls, the seventh argument as another call result, the reserve cursor and
local canary. Success is the line PASS raw64 call7 ABI and exit status zero.

raw64-threads.zero is a BIOS64 fixture for the existing test-loader memory
layout, derived from review-tests/threads/raw64-call.zero. It tests call7
before creating two private worker contexts, 128 rounds of switching,
preserved pending expressions, local slots, reserve cursors and stack guards.
Success writes THREADS OK to port 0xe9 and zero to debug-exit port 0xf4.
Under QEMU isa-debug-exit, that successful device value gives process code 1.

Observed validation

The compiler compiled itself twice to identical host bytes. The independent
candidate2 native builder produced that same compiler. All 182 existing
zero-check cases passed. All 32 additional target/arity cases passed: the
plain and nested call7 fixtures compile on all four raw/EFI targets; the
other six targets reject call7; seven or nine total operands are rejected
for each of the nine named targets. Total operands include the callee.

The native probe and BIOS64 thread fixture both passed. Actual UEFI64
removable-media boot exercised LoadImage using call7, then StartImage,
entered Zero OS, and completed compiler/persistence checks. The other menu
choice loaded the real Flash Writer. The IA32 compiler app is 141673 bytes,
within its 159744-byte bundle slot.

Limits

This verifies the compiler ABI and named test paths, not every firmware.
UEFI calls still require valid caller-owned pointers and ABI-compliant
callees. Firmware stack budgets and nested image lifecycle are loader
responsibilities. The original selfhost-targets expected binary snapshots
are historical artifacts and were not regenerated by this focused change.
